---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import { projects } from '../data/projects';

/* Cards for the featured three, a plain list for the rest (2026-10-07; see
   `featured` in projects.ts). Both keep projects.ts order. */
const featured = projects.filter((project) => project.featured);
const earlier = projects.filter((project) => !project.featured);
---

<Layout>
	<Nav />

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5">
		{/* Hero. The masthead above already carries the name at full size, so this
		    says what the name does and hands off to About — the page's only route
		    there since the nav links came out (2026-08-01). Held to --measure so a
		    wide viewport can't run the line out past a readable length.

		    It used to be the about page's opening line verbatim, matched to its
		    type size so arriving from either direction read as one voice. That
		    stopped being true when this line became its own sentence: the two
		    pages now open differently on purpose, the home page saying what the
		    work IS and About saying who does it. The size match went with it —
		    about-page had followed About's 08-02 step down to the base 19px on
		    THIS line too, and that change was dropped when the two branches were
		    merged, since there is no longer a shared sentence to keep in step.
		    If the two are ever written the same again, re-match the size. */}
		{/* Centred, both lines, each on its own axis (2026-08-02, Hiro). This
		    REPLACES the shared-left-edge lockup from earlier the same day, and the
		    reason it can: that arrangement existed because the second line was a
		    bare text link that had to read as hanging off the sentence, which a
		    second centring axis would have broken. It's a button now — a boxed
		    control is self-evidently its own object, so it doesn't need the
		    sentence's left edge to say so, and the hero reads as one deliberate
		    centred block instead. */}
		{/* Padding stepped up one full stop 2026-08-03 (Hiro: let the hero breathe
		    more) — 64/80 → 96/112, and 80/96 → 112/128 from sm up. Stepped again
		    2026-08-05, same ask and the same way: 112/128, and 128/144 from sm up.
		    The 1rem top/bottom ASYMMETRY is deliberate and survives both
		    increases: the space below has to separate the hero from the projects
		    grid, while the space above only has to clear the nav bar, which is
		    already a ruled edge. Keep stepping BOTH values together if it goes
		    up again — the gap is the point, not the individual numbers. */}
		{/* The hero reveals as ONE block (2026-08-06, Hiro) — the sentence and its
		    button enter together, --i 0 of a two-beat cascade (the projects grid is
		    --i 1). They were two entries (--i 0 and 1, with the cards at +2) until
		    the per-element stagger was flagged against the migaki carve-out —
		    "one entrance on the hero, everything else static" — and collapsed:
		    elements inside a block enter WITH it, same rule the hairline finale
		    already enforces for the card dividers. */}
		<section class="reveal pt-28 pb-32 text-left sm:pt-32 sm:pb-36" style="--i: 0">
			{/* REWRITTEN 2026-09-30 off a hiring-panel read of the site: the old hero
			    was one centred slogan ("Design engineering, with good sense and
			    taste.") over a button, with Hiro's name appearing nowhere on the home
			    page except that button's label and the footer. A recruiter skimming
			    needs name, role, place and what you want inside the first screen, and
			    at 1440×900 the work started below the fold. Hiro chose (from three
			    shapes): left-aligned, name leads, Design Engineer as the title,
			    availability stated as open to any arrangement, slogan dropped from
			    the hero. The slogan still lives in the OG card (scripts/og-card.html)
			    and the og:image:alt in Layout.astro, which describe that image and
			    stay accurate.

			    PADDING IS THE OLD HERO'S, pt-28/pb-32 (sm 32/36), on purpose. The
			    first cut of this rewrite halved it (pt-14/pb-16) to pull the first
			    row of covers into a 900px window, and Hiro pulled it straight back
			    the same day: the block lost its impact with the air gone. Offered a
			    bigger name with partial air instead, they chose same sizes, full air —
			    so the first row starts just under the fold at 1440×900 and the hero
			    floats the way the slogan did. Don't re-cut the padding for the fold
			    argument without re-reading this; it was made and lost.

			    Spacing inside is one rhythm (Hiro, same pass, from three offered):
			    name and role tight as one unit (mt-2), then the SAME gap (mt-6)
			    before the paragraph and before the button, three beats.

			    HISTORY worth keeping from the old line, since the tokens outlive it:
			    --ink-hero was a step PAST body ink, brighter than the wordmark, made
			    for that sentence (2026-08-03); it is still the button's --btn-bg, so
			    it stays. The line ran at w300 and sat 0.125px under the APCA floor in
			    the light theme, a deliberate miss Hiro chose (2026-08-04) — moot now,
			    the heading below is --ink-heading at --weight-emph. */}
			{/* The name is the h1 (the page had no h1 until 2026-08-18; see that
			    note's point about screen readers landing on card h3s). Same classes
			    as the about page's greeting h1 — the site has one heading idiom and
			    this is it, not a display size invented for the front door. */}
			{/* Two-up on sm+ (2026-09-30, third pass, Hiro: "add room for a profile
			    image"): text column left, portrait right, top-aligned, the about
			    page's arrangement brought forward. Chosen over a small avatar beside
			    or above the name. The photo is a PLACEHOLDER box until Hiro picks an
			    image — the detail page's empty-shot idiom (surface-sunken, --line,
			    bg-clip-padding), at 4:5 like the about page's portrait.

			    The photo's width is load-bearing for the prose: the shell's content
			    box is ~913px, the prose line below is ~650px and must not wrap, so
			    photo + gap has to stay under ~263px. The square takes its side from
			    the text column's height (~225px), and the gap is gap-6 (28.5px),
			    not gap-8 — with gap-8 the slack was ~1px. Measured at the 960 shell:
			    column ~660, line 649. Below sm the two stack, text first, photo
			    under the button. */}
			<div class="flex flex-col gap-10 sm:flex-row sm:items-start sm:justify-between sm:gap-6">
			<div class="min-w-0 flex-1">
			<h1 class="text-3xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">
				Hiro Eernisse
			</h1>
			{/* Role over place, two lines by a <br> (Hiro, 2026-09-30, after one
			    pass with them on a single comma-joined line), in body ink a size up
			    from prose so it reads as the heading's subtitle rather than the
			    paragraph's first line. No full stops: these are labels under a name,
			    not sentences. Not an eyebrow: sentence case, no tracking, BELOW the
			    name.

			    leading-snug, NOT the prose's relaxed (2026-09-30, second pass): at
			    1.625 the gap between the two lines was nearly half the gap between
			    beats, and the name+role unit fell apart. Snug closes the pair up so
			    the hero's three beats (name+role, prose, button) read as three. */}
			<p class="mt-2 text-pretty text-base leading-snug text-[var(--ink)]">
				Design Engineer<br />
				San Francisco Bay Area
			</p>
			{/* The site's prose setting (text-sm / leading-relaxed), so this and the
			    about page's paragraphs are the same object. NO width cap of its own
			    (2026-09-30, third pass): it ran at max-w-[20rem] for an hour so it
			    wrapped to two near-equal lines under the short name/role lines, and
			    Hiro called the result too compact — the sentence is to run as ONE
			    line wherever it fits. The text column's width (see the flex row
			    above) is what bounds it now, and that column is sized so this copy
			    (~650px) clears it at the 960 shell with the photo beside it. A
			    longer sentence, or a wider photo, wraps it; re-measure before
			    changing either. "full-time" keeps a NON-BREAKING hyphen (&#8209;)
			    so that when it does wrap (phones, mid widths) "full-" never hangs
			    at a line end.

			    COPY (2026-09-30, fifth pass, Hiro's pick from three): "Product UI and
			    design systems. Open to full-time and freelance work." It replaced
			    "I design and build product UI. Open to full-time, contract and
			    freelance work." for two reasons at once — that one repeated the
			    "Design Engineer" line above it, and at ~650px its full stop ended
			    38px from the photo, so the text read as pressing on the image. The
			    new line is ~68 characters and leaves ~100px clear. "contract" was
			    dropped deliberately; freelance covers it and the word cost the fit.
			    Fragment style, no "I", to match the role and place lines. Two
			    sentences, what the work is and that Hiro is looking, run together
			    on one line (Hiro's call after a pass with them <br>-split; the split
			    moved to the role/place line above instead). */}
			<p class="mt-6 text-pretty text-sm leading-relaxed">
				Product UI and design systems. Open to full&#8209;time and freelance work.
			</p>
			{/* The page's only route to /about, so it's the site's one real button.
			    Label shortened from "About Hiro Eernisse" — the name is the h1 two
			    lines up now, and repeating it in the button read as a form letter.

			    The arrow is DRAWN AT REST and doesn't animate, unlike the project
			    cards' (which appear on hover). Different job: on a card the arrow
			    is a flourish on something already obviously clickable — bordered
			    image, whole-card cursor, a panel that lights up. Here it's the only
			    thing distinguishing a button that navigates from a button that
			    performs an action, and that's information you want before you
			    hover, not after. .btn's flex + gap owns the spacing, so the glyph
			    needs no margin of its own. */}
			<p class="mt-6">
				<a href="/about" class="btn">
					About
					<svg
						viewBox="0 0 24 24"
						fill="none"
						stroke="currentColor"
						stroke-width="2"
						stroke-linecap="round"
						stroke-linejoin="round"
						aria-hidden="true"
						class="inline-block h-[0.85rem] w-[0.85rem]"
					>
						<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
						<polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
					</svg>
				</a>
			</p>
			</div>
			{/* SQUARE (2026-09-30, fourth pass, Hiro: "the image should be a
			    square"), 12rem a side on sm+ — and 12rem = 228px is NOT arbitrary:
			    it is the text column's measured height (name 43 + 9.5 + role pair
			    48 + 28.5 + prose 27 + 28.5 + button 40), so the square's top sits on
			    the name's and its bottom on the button's. The 4:5 it replaced
			    overhung the text by ~36px. IF THE COPY CHANGES HEIGHT (a wrapped
			    prose line, a third role line) THIS NUMBER IS WRONG — re-measure.

			    Tried first: sm:w-auto + sm:self-stretch, letting aspect-ratio derive
			    the width from the stretched height so it could never drift. It
			    collapsed to 2px wide — a flex item's main size is resolved before
			    the stretched cross size is definite, so the ratio has nothing to
			    work from. Don't re-try it. Below sm the side is 11rem. */}
			<div
				class="aspect-square w-[11rem] shrink-0 rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] bg-clip-padding sm:w-[12rem]"
				aria-hidden="true"
			></div>
			</div>
		</section>

		{/* Projects */}
		{/* The second of the page's two hairlines (2026-08-03, Hiro), the first
		    being the nav's own bottom edge. Drawn in global.css (#projects::before)
		    off the shared --rule gradient, which fades both of them out at their
		    ends — so they read as one device: the page is indexed into a bar, an
		    opening statement and a body of work.

		    This one is held to the CONTENT column rather than run full-bleed like
		    the nav's. Different jobs — the nav's line is the underside of a bar
		    that spans the viewport, while this one is a break inside the column,
		    and it lands on the same left edge as the leftmost card's year, the
		    wordmark above it and the copyright below. A full-bleed rule here would
		    read as a second bar rather than as a division of the text block.

		    The space is deliberately lopsided: the hero keeps its full breathing
		    room above the line, and the list opens at pt-12 below it, so the rule
		    belongs to the section it introduces rather than floating between two
		    equal gaps. */}
		<section id="projects" class="scroll-mt-20 pt-12 pb-16 sm:pt-14">
			{/* Visually hidden, and the heading tree is the whole reason it exists
			    (2026-08-18). The cards are <h3> — correct for a card inside a named
			    section, wrong as the first heading under an h1 — so the level this
			    supplies is what makes them correct rather than a skipped level. It is
			    hidden rather than drawn because the section is already introduced by
			    the rule above it (#projects::before): the page indexes itself with a
			    hairline for a sighted reader, and this is that hairline's counterpart
			    for a reader who cannot see it. Drawing it would add a title the design
			    deliberately does without.

			    OUTSIDE the .reveal, which sits on #project-list — same reason the rule
			    is: page-level structure is not part of the content cascade. sr-only is
			    Tailwind's own utility, so this needs nothing in global.css. */}
			<h2 class="sr-only">Projects</h2>
			{/* The grid, the travelling hover panel and the script that drives it
			    all moved into ProjectGrid.astro on 2026-08-10, when the project
			    pages grew a "More projects" strip and needed the same three things.
			    Nothing changed in the move — the rendered markup here is identical
			    — so every comment that used to live at this spot now lives in that
			    component, next to the code it describes.

			    What stays a decision of THIS page: .reveal and --i 1. The grid is
			    the second of the home page's two blocks (the hero is --i 0), and
			    the reveal sits on #project-list rather than on #projects so the
			    section's rule (#projects::before) stays OUTSIDE it — page-level
			    structure enters in the hairline finale, not with the content. See
			    the finale block in global.css, and the Props note in the component
			    for why the caller owns this rather than the grid.

			    THREE ACROSS, NO LOAD MORE (2026-10-07, Hiro, choosing from three
			    layouts). The grid shows only the featured projects now, and three in
			    a two-column grid left Stacksmith alone on a second row. Three
			    columns is the grid the detail pages' More projects strip already
			    runs, measured there (see ProjectGrid.astro), so this is a second
			    consumer of a settled shape, not a new one. The cards are smaller,
			    about 290px, which the two deep studies can afford: the page they
			    lead to is where they get their room.

			    The 2026-08-15 `initial={4}` load-more is gone with it. It deferred
			    whatever ran past two full rows, and with three cards nothing does;
			    the component's own guard would have rendered no button anyway, so
			    dropping the prop removes a number that no longer describes the page.
			    ProjectGrid keeps the feature for any caller that wants it. */}
			<ProjectGrid projects={featured} columns={3} class="reveal" style="--i: 1" />
			{/* ── Earlier work (2026-10-07, Hiro: demote Suma and Shipyard to a
			    text list) ──
			    The projects that are no longer cards. A visible h3 under the
			    section's sr-only h2, set like the detail page's "More projects"
			    heading one step down (text-lg against text-xl): same weight, ink and
			    tracking. Sentence case, no kicker label above it.

			    Each row is a .btn-quiet link, the borderless family the detail
			    page's link-out and the projects toggle already use, so the list
			    adds no new control. --ink-soft at rest, which is the family's own
			    colour and the right register for work the page has chosen to
			    rank below the cards. The arrow is a plain right arrow, drawn like
			    the hero button's, because these stay on the site; the ↗ means
			    leaving it. The dateline beside each is the detail page's
			    year · company, so a reader can place the work without opening it.
			    It stacks under the title below sm and sits beside it from sm up:
			    on a phone a long title pushed only ITS dateline to a second line,
			    so rows disagreed with each other; stacking all of them settles it.

			    Same reveal beat as the grid (--i 1), not a third: the home page's
			    hairline finale is timed to the frame the grid lands, and a third
			    beat would push the content past it. */}
			{earlier.length > 0 && (
				<div class="reveal mt-16" style="--i: 1">
					<h3 class="text-lg [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">
						Earlier work
					</h3>
					<ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 sm:space-y-2">
						{earlier.map((project) => (
							<li class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-baseline sm:gap-x-3">
								<a href={`/projects/${project.slug}/`} class="btn-quiet">
									<span class="btn-quiet__label">{project.title}</span>
									<svg
										class="btn-quiet__arrow"
										viewBox="0 0 24 24"
										fill="none"
										stroke="currentColor"
										stroke-width="2"
										stroke-linecap="round"
										stroke-linejoin="round"
										aria-hidden="true"
										width="14"
										height="14"
									>
										<line x1="4" y1="12" x2="19" y2="12"></line>
										<polyline points="12 5 19 12 12 19"></polyline>
									</svg>
								</a>
								<span class="text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
									<span class="tabular-nums">{project.year}</span>
									{project.company && ` · ${project.company}`}
								</span>
							</li>
						))}
					</ul>
				</div>
			)}
		</section>
	</main>

	{/* text-xs, down from text-sm (2026-08-03, Hiro). A copyright line is the
	    lowest-priority text on the page, and it was sharing a size with real
	    body copy. The projects grid stepped down in the same pass. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
		<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
	</footer>

</Layout>
